    "evidence": [
      {
        "evidence_id": "redirect_current",
        "char_start": 10851,
        "char_end": 11074,
        "quote_sha256": "e919e7f199defa780563dd5b3a002772c39a4320d8fd7856d809d601af41c151",
        "quote": "When comparing client redirection URIs against pre-registered URIs,\n   authorization servers MUST utilize exact string matching except for\n   port numbers in localhost redirection URIs of native apps (see\n   Section 4.1.3).",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "redirect_baseline",
        "char_start": 49125,
        "char_end": 49609,
        "quote_sha256": "5e0ed442dcd57114172ddac22c26a964064169889aa16064fe0029d33795ede7",
        "quote": "When a redirection URI is included in an authorization request, the\n   authorization server MUST compare and match the value received\n   against at least one of the registered redirection URIs (or URI\n   components) as defined in [RFC3986] Section 6, if any redirection\n   URIs were registered.  If the client registration included the full\n   redirection URI, the authorization server MUST compare the two URIs\n   using simple string comparison as defined in [RFC3986] Section 6.2.1.",
        "record_id": "rfc6749"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "bearer_query_current",
        "char_start": 46008,
        "char_end": 46124,
        "quote_sha256": "0ef497f6a9ecb982abf06743b084b7b7a2f87a7f0fcbf3f2b67c3d08bfccf7a0",
        "quote": "Clients MUST NOT pass access tokens in a URI query parameter in\n      the way described in Section 2.3 of [RFC6750].",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "bearer_query_baseline",
        "char_start": 14502,
        "char_end": 14881,
        "quote_sha256": "a6c78ff09dec7eefc7d0a484bf4684770735afbdeb1c5fc4dfce23080792dff5",
        "quote": "Because of the security weaknesses associated with the URI method\n   (see Section 5), including the high likelihood that the URL\n   containing the access token will be logged, it SHOULD NOT be used\n   unless it is impossible to transport the access token in the\n   \"Authorization\" request header field or the HTTP request entity-body.\n   Resource servers MAY support this method.",
        "record_id": "rfc6750"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "public_refresh_current",
        "char_start": 17915,
        "char_end": 18038,
        "quote_sha256": "8e4fcc0d054f48eec1a0899b50f4e7533194f0a311c5382bf96bcf8b49d8837b",
        "quote": "Refresh tokens for public clients MUST be sender-constrained or use\n   refresh token rotation as described in Section 4.14.",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "public_refresh_baseline",
        "char_start": 128309,
        "char_end": 129013,
        "quote_sha256": "69dbef3531d3b622f589b3cd5ec29fc98cd2261db2f2847d94c39a0e8c9687c7",
        "quote": "Refresh token rotation is intended to automatically detect and\n   prevent attempts to use the same refresh token in parallel from\n   different apps/devices.  This happens if a token gets stolen from the\n   client and is subsequently used by both the attacker and the\n   legitimate client.  The basic idea is to change the refresh token\n   value with every refresh request in order to detect attempts to\n   obtain access tokens using old refresh tokens.  Since the\n   authorization server cannot determine whether the attacker or the\n   legitimate client is trying to access, in case of such an access\n   attempt the valid refresh token and the access authorization\n   associated with it are both revoked.",
        "record_id": "rfc6819"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "public_pkce_current",
        "char_start": 13298,
        "char_end": 13388,
        "quote_sha256": "12978a63068e2978b1cefda5d8b22a174fe807d63cd43f110d8192f791b5e31e",
        "quote": "Public clients MUST use PKCE [RFC7636] to this end, as motivated\n      in Section 4.5.3.1.",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "pkce_s256_dependency",
        "char_start": 17483,
        "char_end": 17604,
        "quote_sha256": "389fcccc185b3ed302ab32180a8e16e5c0c73cc351884335415c274a12e2bdec",
        "quote": "If the client is capable of using \"S256\", it MUST use \"S256\", as\n   \"S256\" is Mandatory To Implement (MTI) on the server.",
        "record_id": "rfc7636"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "metadata_current",
        "char_start": 22471,
        "char_end": 22707,
        "quote_sha256": "04c15b5b1823108f9206acd6ecfdfe95f7971a722a471c1cbea54b768d06fd18",
        "quote": "It is therefore RECOMMENDED that authorization servers publish OAuth\n   Authorization Server Metadata according to [RFC8414] and that clients\n   make use of this Authorization Server Metadata (when available) to\n   configure themselves.",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "metadata_issuer_dependency",
        "char_start": 30914,
        "char_end": 31154,
        "quote_sha256": "b00c8d78c32bd5c52961613251782bdb6a75a86ea240f53e420a3d892d88adf2",
        "quote": "the client MUST ensure that the\n   issuer identifier URL it is using as the prefix for the metadata\n   request exactly matches the value of the \"issuer\" metadata value in\n   the authorization server metadata document received by the client.",
        "record_id": "rfc8414"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "multi_as_current",
        "char_start": 12228,
        "char_end": 12372,
        "quote_sha256": "6e983fe72e279920aa81cf834faf2b64c15a9906de10a70dcfb3be661a9938c1",
        "quote": "When an OAuth client can interact with more than one authorization\n   server, a defense against mix-up attacks (see Section 4.4) is\n   REQUIRED.",
        "record_id": "rfc9700"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "issuer_validation_dependency",
        "char_start": 9455,
        "char_end": 9618,
        "quote_sha256": "40596b8ded2cb9a8b6854b69f28c40734c90dc87759ebccd89da74fcc26dc6f1",
        "quote": "If the value does not match the expected\n   issuer identifier, clients MUST reject the authorization response and\n   MUST NOT proceed with the authorization grant.",
        "record_id": "rfc9207"
      }
    ]
  },
